Sprinting state is tracked in DataTracker on the client and is **NOT affected by the keepAttributes flag**:
PlayerEntity:
```java
public boolean isSprinting() {
    return this.getFlag(3);
}
protected boolean getFlag(int index) {
    return ((Byte)this.dataTracker.get(FLAGS) & 1 << index) != 0;
}
```
However, movement speed **IS** stored in attributes and usually updated when changing sprint.
PlayerEntity:
```java
public float getMovementSpeed() {
        return (float)this.getAttributeValue(EntityAttributes.MOVEMENT_SPEED);
}
public void setSprinting(boolean sprinting) {
    super.setSprinting(sprinting);
    EntityAttributeInstance entityAttributeInstance = this.getAttributeInstance(EntityAttributes.MOVEMENT_SPEED);
    entityAttributeInstance.removeModifier(SPRINTING_SPEED_BOOST.id());
    if (sprinting) {
        entityAttributeInstance.addTemporaryModifier(SPRINTING_SPEED_BOOST);
    }

}
```
Entity: (called via super.setSprinting() from EntityPlayer#setSprinting())
```java
public void setSprinting(boolean sprinting) {
      this.setFlag(3, sprinting);
  }
```

So basically what happens in the client is the attributes (such as movement speed) are reset to non-sprinting state but the flag for sprinting is kept in the client. This explains the fov change you see. You are now sprinting with walking speed.

A workaround for grim could be manually resyncing the metadata to get the client out of this "broken sprint" state?

